# Test: deleting the PARENT `ddos` config block takes the live drop rule out of
# the kernel. The removal is read back from nftables while the flood is still
# running.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-ddos-timing-leaves-reach-no-worker AC-6's premise -- a drop
#            rule ddos local installs is always reachable by something that can
#            remove it. The plugin withdraws on its own exit path when it is
#            stopped without being told anything (register.go runEngine's defer
#            -> retireResponder -> responder.go withdrawMitigation).
# PREVENTS:  the orphan that this operator gesture leaves and the sibling gesture
#            does not. `delete ddos local` delivers an empty body for the removed
#            root and the config boundary withdraws on it; `delete ddos` delivers
#            NOTHING. diffMapsRecursive (internal/component/config/diff.go)
#            records ONE removed key for a deleted subtree, so the diff holds
#            "ddos" and nothing under it, and rootHasChanges
#            (internal/component/plugin/server/reload.go) matches only the root
#            itself or a key beneath it -- so ddos-local is not among the
#            affected plugins and gets no verify and no apply. parentRemoved
#            (internal/component/plugin/server/startup_autoload.go) DOES match an
#            ancestor, so the plugin is stopped all the same. The drop then sits
#            in the kernel with no responder to see it and no cap worker to
#            remove it, for the life of the daemon, with no config left that
#            mentions it. The delivery defect itself belongs to
#            internal/component/** and is recorded in
#            plan/journal/component-rebuilt-during-reload.md; this test pins the
#            plugin-side guarantee that holds whatever the reload delivers.
#
# WHY THE KERNEL IS THE ONLY WITNESS. `show ddos local` belongs to the plugin the
# removal stops, so after the commit the daemon has no answer left to give. That
# is also the reading that matters: the orphan is a rule the daemon does not know
# it is enforcing.
#
# WHY NOTHING ELSE COULD REMOVE IT. The flood never stops, so no AttackCleared
# can arrive: the detector needs clear-consecutive-checks evaluations under the
# threshold, which the config puts at 100. And `max-mitigation-duration` is 3600,
# far outside the 40 seconds the assertion allows, so the cap cannot account for
# a removal either. The exit-path withdrawal is the only path left.
#
# WHY COPP IS IN THIS CONFIG. It keeps the FIREWALL engine running after
# ddos-local stops, and without it this test cannot fail. ddos-local declares
# firewall as a dependency, so stopping ddos-local makes firewall a
# dependency-only plugin with no remaining dependents, and the reload stops it
# too (Server.collectOrphanedDependencies). The firewall engine flushes every
# ze-owned table on its own clean shutdown (firewall.FlushAllTables), which
# removes the orphaned drop by accident and hides the defect. Measured in the
# guest for the sibling test on 2026-09-09: with no second dependent, the kernel
# readback passed against code that withdraws nothing. copp declares firewall
# too, so it holds the engine up and the orphan is left where an operator would
# meet it.
#
# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU integration test; NOT executable on the darwin dev host.
#   It needs a fully privileged host and two capability families.
#     - net-admin: ddos-local writes the nftables table, and the probe reads it
#       back through netlink.
#     - bpf (CAP_BPF + CAP_SYS_RESOURCE): the traffic-usage block drives
#       rlimit.RemoveMemlock() and link.AttachTCX()
#       (internal/plugins/trafficusage/attach_linux.go). Without it the victim
#       prefix never resolves, and applyMitigation refuses to install an
#       unscoped rule -- so the test could only fail.
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf
# Serialize against every other ddos test: they all flood the SAME loopback
# interface and the detector picks the victim by top-destination-bytes over that
# interface, so a sibling's concurrent flood is indistinguishable from this
# test's own. Rationale in full: test/plugin/ddos-direction.ci.
option=exclusive:group=ddos-flood
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.detect:value=info
option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.local:value=info
# 50s to install the drop under a flood, then 40s for the commit to land and the
# withdrawal to reach the kernel, plus daemon startup.
option=timeout:value=180s

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
ddos {
	detect {
		enabled true
		absolute-floor 1000
		confirm-duration 1
		clear-consecutive-checks 100
		startup-grace 0
		baseline-window 10
		check-interval 1
		characterize-enable false
	}
	observe {
		incident-ring-size 100
	}
	local {
		response-level enforce
		max-mitigation-duration 3600
	}
}

control-plane-protection {
	bgp {
		rate 100/second
		burst 20
	}
}

traffic {
	usage {
		enabled true
		track-ip true
		interfaces {
			interface lo {
				enabled true
			}
		}
	}
}

plugin {
	external ddos-parent-config-removed-probe {
		run "ze-test fixture plugin/ddos-parent-config-removed-driver"
		encoder json
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=175s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=PARENT-REMOVED-INSTALLED 127.0.0.12
expect=stderr:contains=PARENT-REMOVED-WITHDRAWN 127.0.0.12
expect=stderr:contains=ddos-local: drop rule installed
expect=stderr:contains=ddos-local: the plugin is stopping, removing the drop rule
